{"author":{"id":"6d85cf2fcebaeefd8c5c528f2d5f5f98f26ce3275422d0dc5f7c0fd596f9f59f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791712179,"nonce":"R721bwSq9Udcqb6SG57sbnmk4Ok=","title":"Laying out a page of many short text runs takes 100-240 ms on its first compute","message":"First `compute` after mounting a page made of many short text runs: code blocks highlighted into a run per token, about 1,000 to 2,700 nodes. 1120x780, Apple M-series, release:\n- 1,400 nodes: 194 ms\n- 1,000 nodes: 197-203 ms\n- 2,700 nodes: 225-239 ms\n- 1,060 nodes of mostly prose: about 100 ms\n\nOn a Linux NUC (Iris Xe, another build running alongside), the same page's first frame takes 0.9-1.3 s: a visible stall when the page is opened. Later computes take about 1 ms, so this is shaping for measurement, not the layout algorithm.\n\nSuspects, to profile before choosing a fix:\n- Each text leaf is measured several times per layout (min-content, max-content, then at its final width), and each measure shapes the run again.\n- The shaped-layout cache (256 entries) cannot hold a page of thousands of runs, so the measures for one node evict each other. 5d7e308 found the same thrash on the paint side.\n\nPossible fixes: keep the last shaped result per text node, keyed by content, font, size, spacing and the width asked, and dropped in `Tree::forget`. Or shape a run once unwrapped and derive the min- and max-content widths from it without shaping again. Either must keep measured widths exactly as they are now: layout pixel tests, and blinc_ts's wiki at four scroll depths per page, byte for byte.","files":null}]}